WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.140
CSRF risk is not limited to one Python framework or one kind of page.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:17.080
It can appear in Django, Flask, FastAPI, Pyramid, custom WSGI applications, and internal tools whenever browser-based authentication and state-changing requests meet.

3
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:26.540
The technology stack changes, but the review question remains similar: can a browser send this request with the user's credentials, and does the request change server-side state?

4
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.220
Traditional HTML forms are the familiar case, but they are not the only one.

5
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:41.000
Admin panels, account settings, preference updates, workflow approval screens, and internal dashboards often include state-changing behavior.

6
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:53.580
Because internal tools may be built quickly and used by trusted staff, teams sometimes assume they are lower risk and accidentally skip protections that would be standard in a public-facing application.

7
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.580
Cookie-authenticated APIs also need review.

8
00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:04.120
A Python backend may serve server-rendered pages, JavaScript-enhanced pages, or endpoints used by a frontend.

9
00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:13.340
If the browser automatically sends the user's session cookie, then state-changing API calls may need CSRF protection even when the request body is JSON.

10
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:19.040
The format of the request does not by itself answer whether CSRF is relevant.

11
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.480
Custom routes are a common gap.

12
00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:34.060
A framework may protect standard forms, but a developer may add a custom endpoint, webhook-like path, admin action, or JavaScript route that bypasses the default protection.

13
00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:40.960
Good CSRF review inventories state-changing routes and checks whether the configured protection actually applies to each one.
